      "question": {
        ":question/id": "f2d4fc44-21fb-57b5-8495-1ccc49b51a1f",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
        ":question/problem": "![](images/4e/4e9713958d48a4046546fcd2838f37eab41bd0bcca6efc6e204c845d60eac539.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. If $c=\\lambda a+b,$ then$\\,\\lambda =$\n{{field-1}}.",
        ":question/worked-solution": "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=\\lambda a+b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $b.$ So, we first draw the vector $b$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](images/b2/b2f5f00c7c968d98dd37e2e038b65d00b7cf5accb4aaf2bf2b801f15d3a27082.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the endpoint of the vector $b.$ Then\n\n$$\nc=\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC}.\n$$\n\nIn $\u25b3OPC,$ we have:\n\n- $PC=|a|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PC}$ and $a$ have opposite directions\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PC}=-a.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC} \\\\  & =b-a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=\\lambda a+b=-a+b.$\n\nSince $a\u2226b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $a,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =-1\n$$",
